  "costUsd": null,
  "content": "I'll start by resolving the player.Owen's approach play is uneven against Division I. He is better than the Division I average on short approaches and well behind it from 125 yards out. Strokes gained has no college figure, so that measure can only be read against his team and the Tour.\n\n**Approach proximity (all recorded rounds)**\n- From 50-125 yd he leaves the ball 23.77 ft away. The Division I average is 28 ft, and the Tour is 18 ft. He is ahead of his team average of 28.61 ft and is first of 7 on the team.\n- From 125-175 yd he leaves it 50.58 ft away. The Division I average is 38 ft, a gap of 12.58 ft. His team average is 45.42 ft, and he is last of 7. The Tour is 30 ft.\n- From 175+ yd he leaves it 94.65 ft away. The Division I average is 55 ft, a gap of 39.65 ft. His team average is 79.89 ft. The Tour is 45 ft.\n\n**Strokes gained: approach**\n- Over his last ten rounds he is at -4.16 per round. That window holds 8 rounds with approach data, from June 9 to September 26. There is no college figure for this measure. His team average is -2.33, and he is last of 7. The Tour baseline is 0.\n\n**Greens in regulation**\n- He hit 61.11% of greens over 144 holes in that window. The Division I average is 60%, and his team average is 66.25%. The Tour is 66%.\n- That is level with the Division I average, but fourth of the 7 on the team at best, so his results are not being held back by poor wedge play.\n\nThe loss is in the mid and long irons. His approaches from 125 yd and beyond finish well off the Division I average, and the 50-125 yd shots are his strength. A practice block for him should be built around those longer approaches.",
